If you hold ISO/IEC 42001 and align to the NIST AI RMF, how much of the MAS AI Risk Management Guidelines is already done?

We hand-authored {{rq4.mapping_rows}} mapping rows over {{rq4.expectations}} expectations, counting an unmapped expectation against coverage rather than excluding it.

- ISO/IEC 42001: {{rq4.iso_share}} fully covered
- NIST AI RMF: {{rq4.nist_share}}
- EU AI Act: {{rq4.eu_share}}
- NIST GenAI Profile: {{rq4.genai_profile_share}} (narrow, accurate where it applies)
- Union of ISO + NIST + EU AI Act: {{rq4.union_full_share}}, leaving {{rq4.union_residue_share}} with no full mapping

We pre-registered the prediction that the residue would be the agent and generative-AI clauses. It was refuted. Those are covered. What no framework fully covers is governance machinery and Singapore-specific proportionality: the applicability test and basic regime (§2.2, §2.5), board responsibilities (§3.4), identification and shadow AI (§4.1, §4.4), materiality arbitration (§4.13), third-party accountability (§5.11), independent validation scope (§5.20).

Mapping is interpretive and asymmetric - a management-system standard, product law and voluntary guidance are not commensurable - so {{rq4.low_confidence_rows}} rows are flagged low confidence and listed one by one.

